In <31748@ksr.com> jfw@ksr.com (John F. Woods) writes:

>smc@ve3faq.UUCP (Scott McIntyre) writes:
>>A few weeks ago, I received my registration package for the upcoming school
>>year, and I noticed a new "rule" on the student conduct sheet. The new rule
>>pertained to communication devices. All cellular telephones, pagers, _and
>>two way radio devices_ were banned from being anywhere on school property,
>>for any reason whatsoever. How can they legally stipulate this? I can
>>understand how they would be upset if you took your cell phone or two way
>>radio into class and operated. BUT, do they have any legal grounds to
>>confiscate the equipment and threaten expulsion just because you posess a
>>piece of equipment while being on the property? It was my understanding that
>>the only people who can regulate my operation are the Department of 
>>Communications Canada. It is also my understanding that the only people who 
>>can confiscate my equipment are Communications Canada, law enforcement 
>>agencies, and the Queen.

>Possibly the rules in Canada are different, but this would be fine, here.
>By agreeing to become a student at that college, you agree to whatever bizarre
>arbitrary rules they want to use; if they want you to leave radios at home,
>you gotta leave them at home; if you have to paint yourself bright purple and
>hop to all of your classes, break out the brushes and comfortable shoes.  You
>might try to ask the DOC there, but I'd bet they'll give you the same answer.

[...]

Umm, not completely true.  Accredited institutions of higher learning
have some obligations to students, among them creating an atmosphere
conducive to free thought and learning.  They can't discriminate on the
basis of race, for example, nor can they arbitrarily limit student freedom of
expression (although they can certainly regulate time, place, and
manner, within reason).  It's not clear to me whether this is a
reasonable restriction of time, place, and manner, or an arbitrary one
(i.e. all radio equipment users are criminals unless proven innocent).

Scott also didn't mention whether this was a high school (where students are
presumed to be minors and authority figures are fulfilling a legal
obligation in acting "in loco parentis") or a college (where students
are presumed to be autonomous adults, and have certain rights).  This is
definitely a murky area of law, which defies simple and arbitrary
solutions offered by John and Scott's school.

I know Carl Kadie has commented on radio-related issues before, so I've
crossposted this one to alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk and look forward to
further insights :-).

