No, this is not a good idea.  It's a better idea to write to the
postmaster at the site and explain your concerns.

*Don't* ask to "revoke" his access, or anything like that.  Just point
out what he's posting (feel free to include a sample) and ask if they
have any policy about it it.  End the letter.  Let *them* decide.  At
my previous site (a university), our policy was that people who got
complaints were told to clean up their act.  This was enough for the
most part.  OTOH, my current site actually has a policy of conduct
that you must sign in order to get the account.  

As for those demanding specific examples of people getting kicked off
the net through complaints, it does happen.  I did this inadvertantly
back in '87 or '88 when I complained to a fellow's postmaster that he
was sending me reams of crud (the same letter over 100 times).  He was
suspended from his account for at least a year, but he did get it
reinstated later as I recall.  So it *does* happen, but it is entirely
dependent on the site.  Since then, I have been careful to emphasize
that I am not calling for an account execution, just a friendly chat &
reminder of any local policy if applicable.  Especially .com and .edu
sites will have general policies of conduct.

Censorship flamage aside, I don't think manners -- as in posting
appropriately to different groups -- are too much to ask for from
*everyone*.  Usenet is an anarchy -- it will work only as long as some
very basic ground rules are observed.  So I feel that it's acceptable
to complaint if there is anyone to complain to.

I do suggest that the best course to take is to learn how to use your
kill file.  I'm happy to help with nn and to some extent rn (rn users,
look up Leanne Phillips excellent FAQ on how to kill articles in rn
available in news.answers) if you drop me a private note.

Sigh.  There's a LOT of subjects here I've been dropping into the kill
file...

