From caf-talk Caf Sep  7 18:35:33 1993
Message-ID: <POLITICS%93090208421658@UCF1VM.CC.UCF.EDU>
Date: Thu, 2 Sep 1993 08:41:00 EDT
From: "K. S. Kilburn" <KILBURN@WVNWVSC.BITNET>
Subject:  censorship in education

Hi, y'all:
I watched People for the American Way's press conference about their latest
report on censorship in education.  It was pretty interesting, and thought
y'all might like to hear about it.

This group has been monitoring censorship in K-12 schools for, I guess,
about 11 years.  Although the survey methods weren't discussed extensively,
it looks like the group uses a combination of questionnaires, news reports,
and personal contacts to identify instances of attempted censorship.
Although the folks at the press conference didn't say so explicitly, they
implied that each case of suspected censorship is investigated individually
-- that is, they don't seem to take anyone's word for it.

The man and woman holding the conference (don't remember names) stated that
their operative definition of censorship is the removal of teaching
materials for all students in a class or school.  They stated several times
that they strongly defend the rights of parents to seek alternative
assignments or other opt-outs for their own children for any reason.  Thus,
for example, I could request that my daughter not be required to read
_Catcher in the Rye_ (not that I would), and that wouldn't count as an
attempt at censorship.  Only if I try to get the book pulled from the
library or removed from the relevant curriculum would it count.

On to the results of the survey (which they admit is not comprehensive, and
not designed in such a way as to allow the results to be broadly
generalized to the country as a whole -- which is more than I can say for
folks writing about "political correctness").  In the 92-93 school year,
they identified 395 instances of attempted censorship in 44 states, in
schools of all types (urban and rural, e.g.).  Although the speakers stated
that this represents a rise over the past few years, follow-up questions
quoting some other numbers suggest that the rise may not be very dramatic,
and possibly not statistically significant.

Of the 395 instances, about 340+ involved removal of materials from
classrooms and/or libraries, and 40+ involved sectarian objections to
specific curricula (e.g., sex education, self-esteem programs).  The good
news, I guess, is that "only" 41% of cases resulted in the removal of
materials.  Half of the challenges involved library books (including, but
not limited to, Of Mice and Men, Tom Sawyer, Little House on the Prairie, A
Wrinkle in Time, and The Martian Chronicles).  In about a dozen cases,
teachers were either harrassed, suspended, or fired (including one former
"teacher of the year" -- for attempted to produce the play "Shadowbox", for
which he had already received approval).

Breaking down the challenges by the folks behind them -- 20% of challenges
came from organizations or individuals directly associated with "right-wing
fundamentalist" groups; another 18% appeared to be, but couldn't be so
documented.  Seven percent of challenges came "from the left", and included
challenges to Tom Sawyer (racism) and Little House on the Prairie (negative
portrayal of Native Americans).  These challenges appeared to from
individuals not directly associated with national organizations, in
contrast to the challenges from "the right."

The most common objections were based on religion, including challenges of
self-esteem classes for being "New Age", books like A Wrinkle In Time being
anti-Christian, and (my personal favorite, from some group Phyllis
Schlafley works with) objection to the word "Santa" because it is an
anagram for "Satan."  Religious challenges were followed in popularity,
first by challenges to sex education curricula, and then by challenges to
objectionable language (hells and damns count here).

According to the spokesfolk, censorship attempts are becoming more broad in
their scope over time.  Rather than challenges to individual books, they
said they're seeing more attacks on whole curricula.  Moreover, they're
seeing an increase in the involvement of whole organizations (especially
those of the "religious right"), as opposed to challenges by individual
parents acting "solo".

The only comfort I found as I listened to all of this was from realizing
that maybe, just maybe, these 395 instances were the only ones out there --
maybe they're the exceptions.  What scares me is the possibility that these
are only a small sample of actual cases.  Unfortunately, I had no way of
knowing.  I'm hoping we can get the full report for the library; maybe that
will help.

Kerry
