   I don't have much in the way of first-hand knowledge of such
incidents, but I can comment on your having trouble believing that the
majority of parents share the views that lead to such incidents.
Point 1: in most parts of the US, the schools are administrated on a
small scale -- usually on a county-wide basis: 1 to 5 high-schools and
the affiliated elementary and/or middle schools.  In fact, in many
places the individual schools have an enormous amount of autonomy
(it's called "site-based management" and it's one of the current rages
among professional educators in this country today).  Point 2: in the
US, people expect a LOT more individual choice in their education.
For example, there is NO point in US education where one takes a test
which is used to determine whether one will enter a "college-prep"
track or begin to study a trade -- it's one's own choice (although
financial contraints do play a part in determining who can actually
attend what colleges).
   Put those two together, and what you have is a system that is
EXTREMELY responsive to the efforts of individual parents.  What
usually happens, I believe, in these cases is that a small number of
parents become very vocal about a particular book or program they
believe whould be censored.  The school administrators have nothing to
gain from opposing them (in fact, they might even agree!), so they
take account of such pressure.  And it needn't be only 2 or 3 parents:
if 2 or 3 people mobilize their entire congregation or set of friends
to complain to the schools, then the schools really HAVE to listen
because even such a small group of people form a substancial fraction
of the population that the school serves.  Notice that few of these
were adopted by school districts in major cities: there the
constituancy is large enough that one group of (as you put it)
"morons" is not a major force.
